 ## Background
-The distinction between coupled and decoupled appearance modeling has become a critical design choice in recent Neural Implicit Representation (INR) research, particularly in the context of RGB-D SLAM and real-time rendering. Early NeRF architectures implicitly coupled geometry and appearance through shared positional encodings, but explicit decoupling was introduced to handle view-dependent effects more robustly [S1].
-
-Recent benchmarking work has highlighted that the choice of structure significantly affects the initialization phase of INRs trained on single posed RGB-D frames. While coupled structures accelerate SDF convergence, they can hinder color accuracy in high-resolution dense representations. Conversely, decoupled structures are preferred for hybrid representations that incorporate structural elements like multi-resolution grids or tri-planes, where high-dimensional features are assigned to spatial partitions [S1].
+The distinction between coupled and decoupled appearance modeling is a critical design choice in Neural Implicit Representation (INR) research [S1]. Recent benchmarking work has highlighted that the choice of structure significantly affects the initialization phase of INRs trained on single posed RGB-D frames [S1]. While coupled structures accelerate SDF convergence, they can hinder color accuracy in high-resolution dense representations [S1]. Conversely, decomposition methods benefit from decoupled structures, showing enhanced geometric and appearance fidelity [S1].
 
